 ## Background
 
-*   **Origin:** 3DGSR was introduced by Xiaoyang Lyu, Yang-Tian Sun, Yi-Hua Huang, Xiuzhe Wu, Ziyi Yang, Yilun Chen, Jiangmiao Pang, and Xiaojuan Qi from The University of Hong Kong and Shanghai AI Lab [S1].
-*   **Publication:** The method was published in ACM Transactions on Graphics (SIGGRAPH Asia 2024) [S1].
-*   **History:** The method addresses the limitations of vanilla 3DGS, which optimizes Gaussians for view synthesis but lacks explicit geometric structure, making surface extraction difficult [S1]. It builds upon 3DGS [Kerbl et al. 2023] and neural implicit SDF methods [Wang et al. 2021; Yariv et al. 2021], combining the rendering efficiency of 3DGS with the geometric precision of implicit fields [S1]. It contrasts with Gaussian-based reconstruction methods like 2DGS [Huang et al. 2024], which use 2D Gaussians and may suffer from oversmoothing or higher chamfer errors [S1].
+**Origin:**
+3DGSR was introduced by Xiaoyang Lyu, Yang-Tian Sun, Yi-Hua Huang, et al. from The University of Hong Kong and Shanghai AI Lab [S1]. The paper was published in ACM Transactions on Graphics (SIGGRAPH Asia 2024) [S1].
+
+**History and Development:**
+The method addresses the limitations of vanilla 3DGS, which optimizes for photorealistic rendering but lacks explicit geometric structure, making mesh extraction difficult [S1]. Previous Gaussian-based methods like 2DGS [Huang et al. 2024] suffered from oversmoothed reconstructions and higher Chamfer-L1 errors [S1]. Neural implicit methods [Wang et al. 2021; Yariv et al. 2021] provided good geometry but were slow to train and render [S1]. 3DGSR bridges this gap by coupling an implicit SDF with explicit Gaussians, leveraging the strengths of both [S1].
+
+**Usage:**
+Used for implicit surface reconstruction in 3D Gaussian Splatting pipelines [S1].
 
